Answer: The program has three independent activities: (1) adding two numbers taken from the user with Scanner, (2) printing the characters A to E with a delay of 5 seconds for each character, and (3) printing the numbers 1 to 5. Although the three activities are independent of each other, they are written one below the other, so they run sequentially. Printing characters cannot start until the user has entered both numbers, and printing numbers cannot start until all the characters, with their delays, are finished. While the program waits for the user's input and during every 5-second delay, the CPU time is wasted. This is called sequential execution.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
public class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
// Activity 1: adding
Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
System.out.println("Enter number 1");
int num1 = sc.nextInt();
System.out.println("Enter number 2");
int num2 = sc.nextInt();
System.out.println("Sum = " + (num1 + num2));
System.out.println("Adding completed");
// Activity 2: printing characters (5 seconds delay for each)
System.out.println("Printing characters started");
for (int i = 65; i <= 69; i++) {
System.out.println((char) i);
Thread.sleep(5000);
}
System.out.println("Printing characters completed");
// Activity 3: printing numbers
System.out.println("Printing numbers started");
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
System.out.println(i);
}
System.out.println("Printing numbers completed");
}
}
```

Sample input: 10 and 20. Output (each character appears 5 seconds after the previous one): Enter number 1 Enter number 2 Sum = 30 Adding completed Printing characters started A B C D E Printing characters completed Printing numbers started 1 2 3 4 5 Printing numbers completed

Answer: Each independent activity is moved into the run() method of its own class that extends Thread. In main, an object of each class is created and start() is called on it. Because the three activities are now independent threads, the program asks for the numbers while the characters are already being printed, and the numbers are printed without waiting for the characters. The order of the output lines can vary from run to run, because the thread scheduler decides which thread runs when.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
class Adding extends Thread {
public void run() {
Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
System.out.println("Enter number 1");
int num1 = sc.nextInt();
System.out.println("Enter number 2");
int num2 = sc.nextInt();
System.out.println("Sum = " + (num1 + num2));
System.out.println("Adding completed");
}
}
class PrintingCharacters extends Thread {
public void run() {
System.out.println("Printing characters started");
for (int i = 65; i <= 69; i++) {
System.out.println((char) i);
try {
Thread.sleep(5000);
```

