}
System.out.println("Printing characters completed");
}
}
class PrintingNumbers extends Thread {
public void run() {
System.out.println("Printing numbers started");
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
System.out.println(i);
}
System.out.println("Printing numbers completed");
}
}
public class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Adding a = new Adding();
PrintingCharacters b = new PrintingCharacters();
PrintingNumbers c = new PrintingNumbers();
a.start();
b.start();
c.start();
}
}
```

Answer: If you call run() directly on the object, it is an ordinary method call: its stack frame is created in the main stack, the three calls execute one after the other, and the program runs sequentially (no multithreading). If you call start(), the JVM registers the thread, creates its own small stack, and run() executes in that thread's stack, independently, under the control of the thread scheduler. So you must call start(), and the JVM calls run() for you. Calling run() directly is a wrong way and is a common interview scenario. a.run(); // wrong: sequential, executes in the main stack

```java
b.run();
c.run();
```

a.start(); // correct: a new small stack is created, run() executes there

```java
b.start();
c.start();
```
